      "The equilibrium price is the price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. Above it there is a surplus, and sellers cut prices to clear unsold goods; below it there is a shortage, and buyers bid prices up. If demand rises while supply stays the same, both the equilibrium price and quantity rise. If supply rises while demand stays the same, the price falls and the quantity rises.",
      "Governments sometimes set legal limits on prices. A price ceiling set below the equilibrium price, such as a cap on rents, creates a shortage because more is demanded than supplied at the capped price. A price floor set above equilibrium, such as a minimum price for a crop, creates a surplus. A ceiling above equilibrium or a floor below it has no effect, because it does not bind."
