      "In perfect competition many small firms sell an identical product, buyers and sellers are well informed, and entry and exit are free. No single firm can affect the market price, so each is a price taker. If firms earn economic profits, new firms enter and drive the price down until, in the long run, economic profit is zero and the price equals the lowest average cost of production.",
      "A monopoly is a single seller of a product with no close substitutes, protected by barriers to entry such as control of a key resource, patents, government licenses or large economies of scale. A monopolist is a price maker: it chooses the output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost and charges the highest price buyers will pay for that output. Compared with a competitive market it produces less and charges more, causing a deadweight loss, a loss of gains from trade that no one receives.",
      "In monopolistic competition many firms sell differentiated products, such as restaurants or clothing brands, so each has some control over its price, but free entry pushes long-run economic profit toward zero. In an oligopoly a few large firms dominate, such as in commercial aircraft manufacturing. Because each firm's best choice depends on what its rivals do, oligopolists are interdependent. They can gain by colluding to restrict output and raise prices, but each member of a cartel is tempted to cheat by producing more, and in many countries such agreements are illegal."
