      "Every choice has an opportunity cost: the value of the best alternative given up. A worker who spends an hour making cloth gives up whatever else that hour could have produced. Opportunity cost, not money price alone, is the key to understanding why trade pays.",
      "A producer has an absolute advantage in a good if it can make more of it with the same resources. It has a comparative advantage if it can make the good at a lower opportunity cost than others. David Ricardo set out the idea in 1817 using England and Portugal trading cloth and wine: Portugal could make both goods with less labor, yet both countries gained by specializing and trading.",
      "Suppose a worker in country A can make 10 tons of wheat or 5 bolts of cloth in a month, and a worker in country B can make 4 tons of wheat or 4 bolts of cloth. A has an absolute advantage in both goods. But one bolt of cloth costs A 2 tons of wheat and costs B only 1 ton, so B has the comparative advantage in cloth and A has it in wheat. If each specializes in its comparative-advantage good and they trade at a price between 1 and 2 tons of wheat per bolt, both can consume more than they could alone.",
      "Gains from trade for a country as a whole do not mean everyone gains. When a country opens to imports, consumers and exporting industries usually benefit, while workers and firms in industries that compete with imports can lose. Whether and how to help those who lose is a policy question that the theory itself does not settle."
