    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about how gross domestic product is defined, measured and used. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Gross domestic product, or GDP, is the market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a given period, usually a quarter or a year. It is the most widely used measure of the size of an economy.",
      "Only final goods count. The steel and tires sold to a carmaker are intermediate goods: their value is already included in the price of the finished car, so counting them separately would count the same production twice. GDP also leaves out sales of used goods, which were counted when first produced, purely financial transactions such as buying shares, and government transfer payments such as pensions, because none of these is new production.",
      "GDP can be measured by adding up spending on final output: consumption by households (C), investment by firms in equipment, buildings and inventories (I), government purchases of goods and services (G), and net exports, exports minus imports (NX). So GDP = C + I + G + NX. Imports are subtracted because they were produced in other countries.",
      "Nominal GDP values output at current prices, so it can rise just because prices rise. Real GDP values output at the prices of a fixed base year, so it changes only when the quantity of output changes; growth in real GDP is the usual measure of economic growth. GDP per person is a rough guide to average living standards, but it leaves out unpaid household work, leisure, environmental damage and how income is distributed."
