    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about what inflation is, how it is measured, what causes it and whom it affects. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Inflation is a sustained rise in the general level of prices, so that each unit of money buys less. A rise in the price of one good, such as fuel, is not by itself inflation; inflation means prices across the economy are rising on average. A sustained fall in the general price level is deflation.",
      "Inflation is commonly measured with a consumer price index, or CPI. Statisticians price a fixed basket of goods and services that a typical household buys and compare its cost with its cost in a base year. The inflation rate is the percentage change in the index over a period: if the CPI rises from 200 to 206 in a year, inflation is 3 percent.",
      "In the short run, inflation can rise when total spending grows faster than the economy's capacity to produce, called demand-pull inflation, or when production costs such as energy rise sharply, called cost-push inflation. Over the long run, economists widely agree that sustained high inflation results from the money supply growing much faster than real output. Extreme cases, with prices rising more than 50 percent a month, are called hyperinflation.",
      "Inflation matters because nominal values, measured in money, can mislead. The real interest rate is approximately the nominal interest rate minus the inflation rate. Unexpected inflation transfers purchasing power from lenders to borrowers, because loans are repaid in money worth less than expected, and it hurts people whose incomes are fixed in money terms. Inflation also imposes costs of changing prices and makes planning harder."
    ],
