      "An externality is a cost or benefit that falls on people outside a transaction. A factory whose smoke harms nearby residents creates a negative externality: because the factory does not pay that cost, its product is overproduced relative to the efficient amount. A person who is vaccinated also protects others from infection, a positive externality: because the person does not capture that benefit, vaccination tends to be underconsumed.",
      "Remedies try to make decision-makers count the external effects. A corrective, or Pigouvian, tax set equal to the external cost raises the private cost of a harmful activity; a subsidy can encourage an activity with external benefits. Regulation can set limits directly, and tradable permits cap total pollution while letting firms trade the right to pollute. Ronald Coase argued that if property rights are clear and bargaining is cheap, the affected parties can negotiate an efficient outcome themselves, though this often fails when many people are involved.",
