      "In a plurality system, often called first-past-the-post, the country is divided into single-member districts and the candidate with the most votes in each district wins, even without a majority. It gives each district a clear local representative and often produces single-party majorities, but a party can win a large share of the national vote and few seats if its support is spread thinly, and votes for losing candidates elect no one. Some systems instead require a majority, using a second round between the top candidates or ranked ballots.",
